{"id":"gameliftstreams","corpus":"aws","title":"Amazon GameLift Streams","sourceUrl":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/gameliftstreams/latest/developerguide/what-is-service.html","license":"AWS Documentation © Amazon Web Services, Inc.","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"Amazon GameLift Streams"},{"kind":"p","text":"With Amazon GameLift Streams, game publishers and others can provide on-demand, low-latency streaming experiences to players and viewers globally. Amazon GameLift Streams uses its own streaming technology combined with the AWS global infrastructure to operate and maintain application streaming at scale. Publishers have the flexibility to provision both on-demand and reserved streaming resources to effectively manage capacity and costs."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Features"},{"kind":"p","text":"Amazon GameLift Streams offers these key features:"},{"kind":"li","text":"Streaming technology that delivers real-time gameplay experiences with minimal player-to-cloud latency to any device with a browser using the AWS global footprint."},{"kind":"li","text":"Seamless play of games at high definition (1080p) resolution and 60 fps without requiring downloads, turning any browser-based device into a powerful gaming machine."},{"kind":"li","text":"Scaling tools to adjust your streaming capacity to meet customer demand. For example, with these tools you can keep game streaming costs in line while maintaining enough capacity to accommodate new players into stream sessions quickly."},{"kind":"li","text":"Stream performance analysis using the Amazon GameLift Streams console to track metrics, view stream logs, and review data on stream resource usage."},{"kind":"li","text":"Direct streaming of Windows and Linux-based games with little to no modification."},{"kind":"li","text":"Amazon GameLift Streams SDK to help you integrate your existing identity services, storefront, and client applications."},{"kind":"li","text":"Stream URLs to share a stream session, with no client integration required. A stream URL opens a stream session in a supported web browser, so end users can start streaming without your own web client and without AWS credentials of their own. For more information, see Share stream sessions with stream URLs."},{"kind":"h2","text":"How to get started with Amazon GameLift Streams"},{"kind":"p","text":"If you're a first-time Amazon GameLift Streams user, we recommend that you begin with the following topics:"},{"kind":"li","text":"Setting up Amazon GameLift Streams as a developer covers one-time setup tasks, including getting an AWS account with user access and setting up the software you need for development with Amazon GameLift Streams."},{"kind":"li","text":"Starting your first stream in Amazon GameLift Streams guides you through the critical steps in the content streaming workflow. Starting with your content, such as a game build, you will provision Amazon GameLift Streams streaming cloud resources and initiate a streaming session."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Accessing Amazon GameLift Streams"},{"kind":"p","text":"You can create, access, and manage your application content and streaming resources with the following tools:"},{"kind":"li","text":"AWS Management Console – Provides a web interface that you can use to create and manage your Amazon GameLift Streams applications and stream groups."},{"kind":"li","text":"AWS Command Line Interface (AWS CLI) – Provides commands for a broad set of AWS services and is supported on Windows, Mac, and Linux. For more information on this tool, see the AWS Command Line Interface page."},{"kind":"li","text":"AWS SDK – Provides language-specific APIs and takes care of connection details, such as calculating signatures, handling request retries, and error handling. For documentation on the Amazon GameLift Streams service API, see the Amazon GameLift Streams API Reference. For more general information on the AWS SDK, see Tools to Build on AWS."},{"kind":"p","text":"For additional information on supported AWS Regions, see Regions, quotas, and limitations."}],"lemmas":{"amazons":["amazon","amazons"],"streams":["stream","streams"],"streamed":["streamed","stream"],"publishers":["publisher","publishers"],"others":["others","other"],"provided":["provided","provide"],"providing":["providing","provide"],"demands":["demand","demands"],"demanded":["demanded","demand"],"demanding":["demanding","demand"],"lows":["low","lows"],"lower":["lower","low"],"lowest":["lowest","low"],"latencies":["latency","latencies"],"streaming":["streaming","stream"],"streamings":["streaming","streamings"],"experiences":["experience","experiences"],"experienced":["experienced","experience"],"experiencing":["experiencing","experience"],"players":["player","players"],"viewers":["viewer","viewers"],"globallies":["globallies","globally"],"uses":["use","uses"],"used":["used","use"],"its":["its","it"],"owns":["owns","own"],"owned":["owned","own"],"owning":["owning","own"],"technologies":["technology","technologies"],"combined":["combined","combine"],"combineds":["combineds","combined"],"combines":["combine","combines"],"combining":["combining","combine"],"globals":["globals","global"],"infrastructures":["infrastructure","infrastructures"],"operates":["operates","operate"],"operated":["operated","operate"],"operating":["operating","operate"],"maintains":["maintains","maintain"],"maintained":["maintained","maintain"],"scales":["scale","scales"],"scaled":["scaled","scale"],"flexibilities":["flexibility","flexibilities"],"provisions":["provision","provisions"],"provisioned":["provisioned","provision"],"provisioning":["provisioning","provision"],"reserved":["reserved","reserve"],"reserveds":["reserveds","reserved"],"reserves":["reserve","reserves"],"reserving":["reserving","reserve"],"resources":["resource","resources"],"effectivelies":["effectivelies","effectively"],"manages":["manages","manage"],"managing":["managing","manage"],"managed":["managed","manage"],"capacities":["capacity","capacities"],"costs":["cost"